> Ok but seriously if you can then you should install a newer version than
> 3.2.
> At the >>> prompt where you typed boot device... type
> boot -flag s
> That will pass the 's' flag to the booting kernel which means stop at
> single user mode.
> When you get to single user mode you should get a root shell. However all
> of your disks will be mounted read only.
> Use the command 'bcheckrc' to check and mount all of the disks, then use
> the passwd command to set root's password.
> When you are done, shutdown back to the >>> prompt with init 0 or halt
> whichever takes your fancy.
> Test that the system will boot all the way up to multi-user mode and that
> you can log in with the command boot at the >>> prompt.
> If you can then you can avoid the whole >>> boot process by setting the
> console variable 'auto_action' to boot.
> This needs to be done at the >>> prompt so shut-down again and change it.
