It sounds like the system has a SCSI host adapter with dual channels, both wide
and narrow, maybe an Adaptec 3940 or Adaptec 29160, or some QLogic adapter
perhaps.

There is no problem whatsoever having wide devices on one channel and narrow on
the other.

If the host adapter has only a single wide channel, narrow devices may be set up
on it using the device IDs 0 through 7, and with a smallish adapter that allows
connection of a 50-pin narrow device to a 68-pin wide ribbon cable.  The wide
devices are not as constrained as to device IDs, which may range from 0 through
15.  SCSI device IDs must be unique on a single SCSI bus, whether wide or
narrow.

The additional 18 wires on a wide SCSI cable (compared to narrow) transmit the
additional data bits, SCSI ID bit, and other control bits of information.  Each
device on a SCSI bus is configured individually, so that it is possible to have
devices of mixed speeds and bus width without any degradation in speed of the
faster devices... Ben Myers

