I had a 4000/90A with 4x4 and a 4000/90 with 4x16.  I had some extra 
4-MB SIMMs.  I put the 4x16 into the 90A along with the 4x4 which was 
already there, giving it 80 (one has to put the 16-MB SIMMs in group 0; 
the 4-MB SIMMs had been there so I moved those to group 1).  I then 
filled up the 4000/90 with my spare SIMMs, giving it 32.

I've never changed the memory configuration of a system before.

Both systems showed the new memory correctly at the console.  When VMS 
came up on the 90A, it showed just 64 MB, not 80.  After an AUTOGEN, it 
showed 80 correctly.  The 90, however, showed 32 both at the console and 
with VMS.  I used the same system disk for both, i.e. after I 
reconfigured the 90A for 80 MB, I booted the 90 from it, and VMS showed 
the new configuration without an AUTOGEN.

Of course, when actually using the system, rather than just testing, I 
would do an AUTOGEN regularly anyway, especially after changing the 
memory configuration, whether it is "needed" or not.  However, out of 
curiosity, does VMS automatically detect decreased memory but not 
increased memory?  (This is VMS 7.1 I was using for tests; I had an old 
RZ25 with 7.1 on it and it boots fast, so that seemed OK for testing.  
Whatever the answer to the above question, has the behaviour changed now 
that we have 7.3?)



