>> (Michael Kraemer) wrote:
>> 
>> 
>> >In which way do I "steal" IP from HP this way ?
>> >I buy used equipment from somebody how as bought it from
>> >somebody else who has paid money to HP for the fresh equipment.
>> >If I buy a used car, do I "steal" it from GM ?
>> >
>> >Now if I replicated the stuff and sold it for own account,
>> >that would be theft, of course.
>> 
>> Because the fellow you bought the system from didn't own the software.  He
>> owned a license to use it under specific terms.  Those terms typically do
>> no include the right to transfer the license to a 3rd party (you), or to
>> extend the license to multiple parties.
>> 
>> HP owns the software.  To use it legally, you need HP's permission, in the
>> form of a license.
>
>And all this is exactly what only IP lawyers and brainwashed politicians
>find "legal" or "natural". If I buy a product, e.g. a car, it is
>mine and I can do withit whatever I like (except pirating of course).

No, you can't do whatever you like.  You can't reverse engineer the car
and make copies of it, or of its components.  (It wouldn't matter,
legally, whether the copies were for personal use or for sale to others.) 

This is seldom a big issue for cars, since it costs a lot to make illegal
copies.  Very few people try it, and the impact of ignoring the few who do
is minimal.  High-volume counterfeiters are routinely tracked down and
prosecuted.

>Volkswagen or GM or BMW has no say anymore, and rightfully so.

They certainly do have a say.  If you start making enough illegal copies
of their patented parts to attract their notice, they will send lawyers to
stop you.  The law is very clear.

>Why should a software product be treated differently ?

Except for the details of the laws (copyright vs. patent), software is
treated much the same as cars.  You may buy, lease, or rent physical
objects (cars, computers, CDs, etc.) that make use of intellectual
property.  Doing so does NOT confer any rights to the intellectual
property.

The reason it is a big problem with software is that copying software is
essentially free of cost, unlike copying a car.  

Your point of view seems to be that since it is cheap to steal software,
it should be legal.  That is certainly a valid opinion, but it doesn't
correspond to the laws in effect.  You are free to try to change the laws,
of course.

I'm neither a lawyer nor a politician, but I do find the current
intellectual property laws mostly sensible.


